TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Design","issue":"Launch site latitude for Kourou (CSG) is erroneously stated as 45.05°N; Kourou is located near the equator at approximately 5.23°N."},{"where":"Mission Design","issue":"Semi-major axis calculation error: 6371 km Earth radius + 600 km altitude is calculated as 7171 km instead of 6971 km."},{"where":"Mission Design","issue":"Sun-synchronous inclination at 600 km altitude is stated as 87.79° (prograde), which is physically invalid for an SSO; the correct retrograde inclination is 97.79° (as shown on the student's plot)."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Text reports an average pass duration of 'almost twenty minutes', contradicting the included STK report which indicates a mean duration of 580.8 seconds (~9.7 minutes)."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag sail discussion terminates abruptly mid-sentence: 'utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable...'."},{"where":"Mission Design","issue":"Direct copy-pasting of raw Wikipedia tables into the document body with unformatted hyperlink reference numbers (e.g. [50][51], [66])."}],"overall":"The proposal introduces an interesting coral reef monitoring mission for Australia. While the high-level architecture covers the necessary mission phases using STK analysis, the submission is compromised by basic technical errors (arithmetic, inclination, Kourou latitude, pass duration) and unpolished presentation, including Wikipedia citations, unformatted tables, and incomplete text.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report presents a relevant mission concept addressing Great Barrier Reef monitoring using fluid lensing. The rationale for deploying a single satellite is clearly justified based on coral bleaching timescales. However, mission users are only defined broadly as marine scientists and Australian ground facilities, without identifying specific operational entities (e.g., ASA, CSIRO) or customer organisations (e.g., GBRMPA). Referencing relies heavily on general online links.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict and react to coral bleaching events."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The mission architecture covers launch, orbit, ground access, and passive de-orbiting using STK. However, multiple technical errors undermine the design: Kourou's latitude is given as 45.05°N instead of 5.23°N; semi-major axis arithmetic is incorrect (6371 + 600 = 7171 km); inclination is transcribed as 87.79° (prograde) rather than 97.79° (retrograde SSO); and STK mean pass duration (581 s / ~9.7 min) is incorrectly stated as 'almost twenty minutes'.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design","quote":"altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of 87.7924° for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Mission Design","quote":"Earth radius of 6371km plus a proposed altitude of 600km as previously explained."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the primary mission decisions across launch, orbit, ground station access, and end-of-life disposal. While it ties together the main operational segments and highlights risks with the unbuilt Vega E vehicle, it remains largely qualitative and does not critically synthesise performance compromises or technical limitations encountered during the design.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"After analysis of each mission segment, a fully described satellite configuration has been constructed."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report follows a standard structure with an executive summary. However, presentation quality is degraded: figures and tables lack standard numbering and captions; Wikipedia tables with raw footnote tags are copy-pasted; a broken sentence appears in the EOL section; reference list formatting is messy with broken entries; and Wikipedia is cited as a source despite being explicitly prohibited.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes [10]."}]}]}
